{
  "mn86:0.1": "Middle Discourses 86 ",
  "mn86:0.2": "With Aṅgulimāla ",
  "mn86:1.1": "So I have heard. ",
  "mn86:1.2": "At one time the Buddha was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. ",
  "mn86:2.1": "Now at that time in the realm of King Pasenadi of Kosala there was a bandit named Aṅgulimāla. He was violent, bloody-handed, a hardened killer, merciless to living beings. ",
  "mn86:2.2": "He laid waste to villages, towns, and countries. ",
  "mn86:2.3": "He was constantly murdering humans, and he wore their fingers as a necklace. ",
  "mn86:3.1": "Then the Buddha robed up in the morning and, taking his bowl and robe, entered Sāvatthī for alms. ",
  "mn86:3.2": "Then, after the meal, on his return from almsround, he set his lodgings in order and, taking his bowl and robe, he walked down the road that led to Aṅgulimāla. ",
  "mn86:3.3": "The cowherds, shepherds, farmers, and travelers saw him on the road, ",
  "mn86:3.4": "and said to him, ",
  "mn86:3.5": "“Don’t take this road, ascetic. ",
  "mn86:3.6": "On this road there is a bandit named Aṅgulimāla. He is violent, bloody-handed, a hardened killer, merciless to living beings. ",
  "mn86:3.7": "He has laid waste to villages, towns, and countries. ",
  "mn86:3.8": "He is constantly murdering humans, and he wears their fingers as a necklace. ",
  "mn86:3.9": "People travel along this road only after banding closely together in groups of ten, twenty, thirty, forty, or fifty. ",
  "mn86:3.10": "Still t